    "https://workforcenow.adp.com/mascsr/default/mdf/recruitment/recruitment.html?cid=7353b4b0-47be-41c8-bcb3-dc2ae3843581&ccId=19000101_000001&jobId=9205199783540_1&lang=en_US": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Agricultural Education",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-06-10\n\nLocation: Huntington\n\nHuntington University has an opening for a tenure track faculty position, Assistant Professor of Agricultural Education.  Primary responsibilities include leading and recruiting for Huntington University's agricultural teacher education program. This position reports to the Director of Haupert Institute for Agricultural Studies and works in close collaboration with the Director of Teacher Education and the Managing Director of the Haupert Institute for recruiting activities and events.  QUALIFICATIONS Must have a strong personal Christian commitment and faith in Jesus Christ and a desire to serve others Must be committed to the mission of Huntington University as a comprehensive Christian College dedicated to educating men and women to impact our world for Christ Demonstrated understanding of Christian higher education and commitment to Christian faith integration in teaching and mentorship Master's degree in Education or Ag Education; or a B.S. with considerable experience in the same; must hold a current teaching licensure for teaching agriculture at the secondary education level Experience teaching agriculture at the high school or middle school level Demonstrated success with engagement in Ag Education and the broader agricultural community Past history of service in FFA or 4-H; experience in agriculture judging events or team management preferred Excellent communication and interpersonal skills Able to work collaboratively with others in a variety of settings Current membership and/or willingness to join professional organizations such as the Indiana Association for Agricultural Educators (IAAE), the National Association of Agricultural Educators (NAAE), the American Association for Agricultural Education (AAAE), or the North American Colleges and Teachers of Agriculture (NACTA) Candidates will be given preferential consideration if they have experience and/or credentials in CTE core pathway topics, including but not limited to: animal, plant, or agribusiness systems, ag leadership and communications, environmental and natural resources, food products and processing, or power and structure systems (ie: ag mechanics, welding, construction, etc.). General Information:   Huntington University is an independent, Christian liberal arts university.  Employees of Huntington University subscribe to the Statement of Faith that is identical to that of the National Association of Evangelicals.   Statement of Faith Work Schedule:  This is a full time, 10-month faculty, exempt position. To Apply: Review the full job description attached for a complete list of responsibilities and qualifications. Apply via ADP, using your personal email, and upload a cover letter, resume and list of references with your completed application. Questions can be emailed to Andy McKee, Director of Human Resources.",
      "department": ""
    },
    "https://workforcenow.adp.com/mascsr/default/mdf/recruitment/recruitment.html?cid=7353b4b0-47be-41c8-bcb3-dc2ae3843581&ccId=19000101_000001&jobId=9205231153975_1&lang=en_US": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Film Production & Capstone Director",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-14\n\nLocation: Huntington\n\nHuntington University has an opening for an Assistant Professor of Film Production & Capstone Director. Under the direction of the Dean of the Arts, the Assistant Professor of Film Production will teach and mentor students within a hands-on, project-based curriculum while continuing professional work that contributes to the growth of the Film & TV Production program. The position will also serve as Director of the Capstone Film Program, leading students in significant feature-length cinematic productions that emphasize intentional storytelling and professional production practices. The focus is on nurturing students’ artistic expression and helping them channel their God-given talents toward successful careers in film. This is an 11-month, tenure-track faculty position. QUALIFICATIONS Strong personal Christian commitment and faith in Jesus Christ and a desire to serve others. Commitment to the mission of Huntington University as a comprehensive Christian university dedicated to educating men and women to impact our world for Christ. Demonstrated understanding of Christian higher education preferred. 5 years of professional film production experience strongly encouraged. Teaching experience in film production, screenwriting, or related areas preferred. Professional experience in areas such as directing, producing, cinematography, post-production, sound, production design, or screenwriting is highly valued. Terminal degree (MFA or Ph.D.) preferred. Strong interpersonal, communication, and organizational skills. Demonstrated ability to collaborate effectively with faculty, students, and industry professionals. Energetic, student-centered teacher and mentor who enjoys working alongside students in production environments. Commitment to continuous improvement, creative innovation, and professional excellence. General Information:   Huntington University is an independent, Christian liberal arts university.  Employees of Huntington University subscribe to the Statement of Faith that is identical to that of the National Association of Evangelicals.   Statement of Faith Work Schedule: This is a full-time, 11-month tenure track exempt faculty position. For more information, please see https://www.huntington.edu/digital-media-arts To Apply: Review the full job description attached for a complete list of responsibilities and qualifications. Apply via ADP, using your personal email, and upload a cover letter, resume and list of references with your completed application. Questions can be emailed to Andy McKee, Director of Human Resources.",
      "department": ""
    },
    "https://workforcenow.adp.com/mascsr/default/mdf/recruitment/recruitment.html?cid=7353b4b0-47be-41c8-bcb3-dc2ae3843581&ccId=19000101_000001&jobId=9205233696554_4&lang=en_US": {
      "title": "Visiting Instructor, Core Liberal Arts & Sciences",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-24\n\nLocation: Huntington\n\nHuntington University has an opening for a full-time Visiting Instructor to teach broadly across our Core4 interdisciplinary curriculum and Forester Foundations general education courses. This is a three-year, grant-funded teaching position running from January 2027 through December 2029. Teaching and meeting with students are the heart of this position. However, this role also requires a successful candidate to be involved in the life of the mind across campus, engaging with both Academic Affairs and Student Life. The position carries no committee assignments and no advising responsibilities within a major, so the person who takes it can give full attention to the classroom and on-campus student experience. Mission Alignment Huntington University's Forester Foundations curriculum asks every core course to help students analyze their subject matter from a Christian perspective. A faculty generalist in this role brings that same faith integration to whatever discipline they're teaching that semester, whether it's a Core4 course built around belonging, creativity, discovery, or flourishing, or a general education course. QUALIFICATIONS Must have a strong personal Christian commitment and faith in Jesus Christ and a desire to serve others. Must be committed to the mission of Huntington University as a comprehensive Christian College dedicated to educating men and women to impact our world for Christ. Demonstrated understanding of Christian higher education and commitment to faith integration in teaching. Master's degree required; earned doctorate (Ph.D.) preferred. Demonstrated excellence in undergraduate teaching, ideally including experience in general education, interdisciplinary, or first-year seminar contexts. Comfort and versatility teaching across disciplines, since this role moves between Core4 and general education coursework rather than staying within a single department. Excellent communication and interpersonal skills, with a genuine interest in mentoring students outside of the classroom. Ability to work collaboratively with faculty and staff across multiple departments and programs. Work Schedule: This is a full-time, 9-month tenure track exempt faculty position.  General Information:   Huntington University is an independent, Christian liberal arts university.  Employees of Huntington University subscribe to the Statement of Faith that is identical to that of the National Association of Evangelicals.   Statement of Faith To Apply: Review the full job description attached for a complete list of responsibilities and qualifications. Apply via ADP, using your personal email, and upload a cover letter, resume and list of references with your completed application. Questions can be emailed to Andy McKee, Director of Human Resources.",
      "department": ""
    },
    "https://workforcenow.adp.com/mascsr/default/mdf/recruitment/recruitment.html?cid=7353b4b0-47be-41c8-bcb3-dc2ae3843581&ccId=19000101_000001&jobId=9205174755563_1&lang=en_US": {
      "title": "Adjunct Professor of Psychology- AZ Location",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-03-03\n\nLocation: Peoria\n\nHuntington University Arizona is seeking an adjunct faculty member to teach PY111 Introduction to Psychology for the spring semester. PY111 is a 3-credit undergraduate course designed to introduce students to the scientific study of behavior and mental processes. This course surveys major principles, methods, and findings in psychology, including development, learning and memory, biological bases of behavior, cognition, social and personality psychology, motivation and emotion, mental health, and ethical practice. Students are also introduced to psychological research methods and the application of psychology through a Christian worldview COURSE OVERVIEW PY111 emphasizes: Foundational psychological concepts and terminology Scientific reasoning and interpretation of psychological research Active student engagement through discussions, applied activities, and research labs Integration of psychology with ethical practice and a Christian worldview Instruction includes lectures, discussion, quizzes, research lab activities, and a comprehensive final exam, with readings delivered through Perusall and Moodle MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS : Master’s degree in psychology or a closely related field (or equivalent professional experience) Teaching experience or demonstrated potential for effective college-level instruction Supportive of a Christian faith environment and the mission of Huntington University Enjoys engaging students through interactive and discussion-based teaching   THE ADJUNCT PROFESSOR WILL BE RESPONSIBLE FOR: Teaching PY111 in accordance with the university catalog, course syllabus, and departmental guidelines Preparing course materials, assessments, and grading student work in a timely manner Providing a course syllabus to students and the division office as requested Maintaining accurate student records and required academic documentation  Communicating promptly and professionally with students, faculty, and staff Supporting student engagement, ethical academic practices, and inclusive classroom learning Performing other related duties as assigned General Information:   Huntington University is an independent, Christian liberal arts university.  Employees of Huntington University subscribe to the Statement of Faith that is identical to that of the National Association of Evangelicals.   Statement of Faith Work Sch edule:  This course meets once per week. To Apply:   Apply via ADP, using your personal email, and include/upload a cover letter, Curriculum Vitae should include name, email address, postal address, phone number, all academic degrees earned with institutions, years attended, conferral dates, and discipline indicated.   Also, include employment since college with dates of service and other relevant experience and names, email and postal address and phone numbers of two professional references.    Questions can be emailed to Andy McKee, Director of Human Resources.",
      "department": ""
    },
    "https://workforcenow.adp.com/mascsr/default/mdf/recruitment/recruitment.html?cid=7353b4b0-47be-41c8-bcb3-dc2ae3843581&ccId=19000101_000001&jobId=9205235016907_2&lang=en_US": {
      "title": "Instructional Technologist",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-28\n\nLocation: Huntington\n\nHuntington University has an opening for a full-time Instructional Technologist Huntington University invites applications for a full-time, 12-month Instructional Technologist in the Center for Academic Technology, part of the new Office of Academic Effectiveness. Reporting to the Dean of Academic Effectiveness, the Instructional Technologist will help faculty, staff, and students use academic technologies effectively, responsibly, and reliably in support of teaching and learning. The Instructional Technologist will also partner with faculty and the Instructional Designer to integrate AI thoughtfully into face-to-face, hybrid, and online courses, with attention to learning goals, assessment, accessibility, privacy, and student support. This is a three-year, grant-funded position supported by the Lilly Endowment AIHE Implementation Grant through December 2029. Huntington University expects to sustain instructional-technology capacity beyond the grant period; therefore, permanent employment may be possible at the conclusion of the grant, based on institutional need, performance, and available funding. QUALIFICATIONS Minimum Qualifications: Must have a strong personal Christian commitment and faith in Jesus Christ and a desire to serve others Must be committed to the mission of Huntington University as a comprehensive Christian College dedicated to educating students to impact our world for Christ  Demonstrated understanding of Christian higher education, preferred Positive interpersonal skills and a team player Excellent communications skills Commitment to continuous process improvement Bachelor's degree in educational technology, instructional technology, information technology, computer science, learning sciences, or a related field; equivalent relevant professional experience may be considered. Experience administering, supporting, or implementing a learning-management system or comparable academic technology platform. Demonstrated ability to troubleshoot technical issues, communicate clearly with nontechnical users, and manage multiple projects and support requests. Working knowledge of technology integrations, user provisioning, data exchange, or related platform-administration processes. Demonstrated fluency with AI tools and their instructional applications, limitations, privacy and security considerations, and implications for assessment and academic integrity. Ability to consult with faculty on selecting and implementing AI-supported learning activities and technologies across face-to-face, hybrid, and online learning environments. Experience facilitating training or providing consultation to faculty, staff, students, or other adult learners. Strong customer-service orientation, interpersonal skills, documentation practices, and attention to detail. Commitment to accessibility, user-centered design, responsible technology use, and data privacy. Preferred Qualifications: Master's degree in educational technology, instructional technology, information systems, or a related field. Experience with Moodle or another major learning-management system. Experience with LMS integrations, LTI tools, learning analytics, or enterprise software support. Familiarity with accessibility standards and practices for digital learning environments. Experience supporting faculty development, course redesign, assessment redesign, or AI literacy initiatives in higher education. Experience applying AI tools to support teaching and learning in face-to-face, hybrid, or online courses. Experience working collaboratively across academic and technology units. General Information:   Huntington University is an independent, Christian liberal arts university.  Employees of Huntington University subscribe to the Statement of Faith that is identical to that of the National Association of Evangelicals.   Statement of Faith Work Schedule:  This is a full-time, exempt, 12-month grant funded position. This position offers work location flexibility and may be performed on campus, fully remotely, or in a hybrid arrangement.  To Apply: Review the full job description attached for a complete list of responsibilities and qualifications. Apply via ADP, using your personal email, and upload a cover letter, resume and list of references with your completed application. Questions can be emailed to Andy McKee, Director of Human Resources.",
      "department": ""
    },
    "https://workforcenow.adp.com/mascsr/default/mdf/recruitment/recruitment.html?cid=7353b4b0-47be-41c8-bcb3-dc2ae3843581&ccId=19000101_000001&jobId=9205235018940_2&lang=en_US": {
      "title": "Instructional Designer",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-28\n\nLocation: Huntington\n\nThe Instructional Designer serves as the Office of Academic Effectiveness' primary specialist in learning design, course development, and faculty partnership. The position collaborates with faculty to create and redesign accessible, engaging, and academically rigorous face-to-face, hybrid, online, and technology-enhanced learning experiences. Particular emphasis will be given to AI-forward course design, assessment practices that make student learning and thinking visible, and faculty development related to responsible use of AI. The Instructional Designer will partner with faculty, the Instructional Technologist, and Information Technology to integrate AI thoughtfully into face-to-face, hybrid, and online courses, with attention to learning goals, assessment, accessibility, privacy, and student support. This is a three-year grant-funded position created through Huntington University's Lilly Endowment AIHE Implementation Grant. Continuation beyond the grant period may be possible based on institutional need, performance, and available funding. Minimum Qualifications: Must have a strong personal Christian commitment and faith in Jesus Christ and a desire to serve others Must be committed to the mission of Huntington University as a comprehensive Christian College dedicated to educating students to impact our world for Christ Demonstrated understanding of Christian higher education, preferred Positive interpersonal skills and a team player Excellent communications skills Commitment to continuous process improvement Bachelor's degree in instructional design, educational technology, learning sciences, curriculum and instruction, or a closely related field. Demonstrated experience designing or substantially redesigning face-to-face, hybrid, online, or technology-enhanced higher-education learning experiences. Knowledge of learning-design principles, including alignment of outcomes, assessments, activities, materials, and technology. Demonstrated fluency with AI tools and their instructional applications, limitations, privacy and security considerations, and implications for assessment and academic integrity. Ability to consult with faculty on selecting and implementing AI-supported learning activities and assessment approaches across face-to-face, hybrid, and online learning environments. Demonstrated knowledge of accessibility and usability principles for digital learning environments. Experience consulting, coaching, or facilitating professional learning with faculty members or other adult learners. Strong project-management, communication, collaboration, and problem-solving skills. Demonstrated facility with learning-management systems and common educational technologies. Preferred Qualifications: Master's degree in instructional design, educational technology, learning sciences, or a related field. Three or more years of instructional-design experience in higher education. Experience with Moodle or another major learning-management system. Experience with Quality Matters, Universal Design for Learning, WCAG/accessibility practices, or comparable course-quality frameworks. Experience designing AI-supported or AI-resilient assignments, assessments, faculty-development programming, or AI-literacy learning experiences. Experience collaborating with instructional technologists, information-technology professionals, or other cross-functional academic technology partners. A professional portfolio demonstrating successful collaborative course-design work. General Information:   Huntington University is an independent, Christian liberal arts university.  Employees of Huntington University subscribe to the Statement of Faith that is identical to that of the National Association of Evangelicals.   Statement of Faith Work Schedule:  This is a full-time, exempt, 12-month grant funded position. This position offers work location flexibility and may be performed on campus, fully remotely, or in a hybrid arrangement.  To Apply: Review the full job description attached for a complete list of responsibilities and qualifications. Apply via ADP, using your personal email, and upload a cover letter, resume and list of references with your completed application. Questions can be emailed to Andy McKee, Director of Human Resources.",
      "department": ""
    }
  },
